 "result": "Under the main root cause as I framed it (Werkzeug 3.0.3 installed against `werkzeug>=3.1.0`), two failures are not caused by it, and one is a different symptom of it.\n\n**Not caused by it**\n\n1. **`tests/test_helpers.py::TestStreaming::test_async_view`**\n   - It raises `RuntimeError: Install Tessel with the 'async' extra in order to use async views.` from `src/tessel/app.py:1096`.\n   - `async_to_sync` fails to import `asgiref`, which isn't installed in this environment.\n   - This is a missing optional dependency, unrelated to Werkzeug.\n\n2. **`tests/test_helpers.py::test_open_resource_exceptions[a]`**\n   - I don't know the cause. It showed as a failure in the full run, with a truncated `pytest.Pytes...` message, and I never saw its traceback.\n   - When I ran it alone, all 4 parametrized cases passed and 1 was skipped, so it may depend on test order or be flaky.\n   - I couldn't rerun the full suite to see its traceback because Bash was denied. I have no evidence tying it to the Werkzeug issue, so treat it as unexplained.\n\n**Same root cause, different symptom**\n\n3. **`tests/test_request.py::test_limit_config`**\n   - It fails at `tests/test_request.py:33` with `assert None == 500000`, because `Request({}).max_form_memory_size` is `None` instead of 500_000.\n   - It is not the `partitioned` `TypeError`.\n   - I attributed it to the old Werkzeug because I believe the 500_000 default for `max_form_memory_size` came with 3.1. I didn't confirm that in Werkzeug's source or changelog, so it is a likely but unverified link.\n\nThe other 21 failures plus the teardown error all come from the `partitioned` `TypeError` at `src/tessel/sessions.py:374`.",
